record.content:
Both the electric voltage and the \PMlinkescapetext{current in a double conductor} satisfy the {\em telegraph equation} \begin{align} f_{xx}''-af_{tt}''-bf_t'-cf = 0, \end{align} where $x$ is distance, $t$ is time and\, $a,\,b,\,c$\, are non-negative constants.\, The equation is a generalised form of the wave equation.
If the initial conditions are\, $f(x,\,0) = f_t'(x,\,0) = 0$\, and the boundary conditions \,$f(0,\,t) = g(t)$,\, $f(\infty,\,t) = 0$,\, then the Laplace transform of the solution function \,$f(x,\,t)$\, is \begin{align} F(x,\,s) = G(s)e^{-x\sqrt{as^2+bs+c}}. \end{align} In the special case\, $b^2-4ac = 0$,\, the solution is \begin{align} f(x,\,t) = e^{-\frac{bx}{2\sqrt{a}}}g(t-x\sqrt{a})H(t-x\sqrt{a}). \end{align}
{\em Justification of} (2).\; Transforming the differential equation (1) gives $$F_{xx}''(x,\,s)-a[s^2F(x,\,s)-sf(x,\,0)-f_t'(x,\,0)]-b[sF(x,\,s)-f(x,\,0)]-cF(x,\,s) = 0,$$ which due to the initial conditions simplifies to $$F_{xx}''(x,\,s) = (\underbrace{as^2+bs+c}_{K^2})F(x,\,s).$$ The solution of this ordinary differential equation is $$F(x,\,s) = C_1e^{Kx}+C_2e^{-Kx}.$$ Using the latter boundary condition, we see that $$F(\infty,\,s) = \int_0^\infty e^{-st}f(\infty,\,t)\,dt \equiv 0,$$ whence\, $C_1 = 0$.\, Thus the former boundary condition implies $$C_2 = F(0,\,s) = \mathcal{L}\{g(t)\} = G(s).$$ So we obtain the equation (2).
